Medium-Relative Composition / Combination Grammar Atlas

Canonical definition

The Combination Grammar Atlas (CGA) is a domain-independent library of the ways multiple elements become a composite. It separates the elements from the carrier, coupling operator, arrangement, timing, receiver, context, and scale; measures how changing any one of these changes the result; extracts the transformation as a reusable invariant; and transduces that operator into other domains where it is tested against reality and a null.

Pantheon handles: Chromatic Algebra, Color Is a Verb, and Mixing Loom.

The carrier observation is color: the same named colors combine differently as emitted light, mixed pigments, stacked filters, adjacent dots, rapid flashes, opponent signals, structural surfaces, fluorescent materials, or signals received by another sensory system. The lesson is not limited to color.

> Elements do not determine their composite. The coupling law does.

Master equation

For components (x_1,\ldots,x_n), medium (m), coupling operator (\Gamma), spatial arrangement (g), timing (\tau), receiver (r), context (c), and resolution/scale (\sigma):

[ Y_{r,c,\sigma}

\mathcal P_{r,c,\sigma}
\left[
\Gamma_m
\left(
{x_i},g,\tau
\right)
\right]
]

where:

• (x_i): components;
• (m): physical, semantic, social, computational, or institutional medium;
• (\Gamma_m): combination grammar licensed by that medium;
• (g): spatial, relational, or topological arrangement;
• (\tau): order, duration, rhythm, and synchronization;
• (\mathcal P): receiver-specific sensing, projection, decoding, and inference;
• (c): local and global context;
• (\sigma): observational scale or resolution;
• (Y): experienced, measured, or actionable composite.

The same components can yield different (Y) because the operation is different. Different components can yield the same (Y) because the receiver compresses them into the same observable state.

