First-order invariants

I. Medium-selects-algebra

There is no context-free combine(a,b). The valid operator depends on what carries the elements.

[
a\oplus_m b \neq a\oplus_{m’}b
]

Before predicting a composite, identify the medium and its allowed operations.

II. Receiver-participation

The receiver is part of the transformation, not a passive terminal.

[
Y_r=\mathcal P_r(X)
]

Two receivers can extract different worlds from the same signal. One receiver can treat different causes as identical.

III. Arrangement-is-an-operator

Adjacency, overlap, nesting, serial order, network position, distance, orientation, and boundary geometry change the composite even when components remain fixed.

[
\Gamma(X,g_1)\neq\Gamma(X,g_2)
]

IV. Timing-is-an-operator

Order and phase matter. Many combination grammars are noncommutative:

[
a\circ b \neq b\circ a
]

Simultaneous, alternating, delayed, rhythmic, and accumulated inputs should be treated as distinct formations.

V. Resolution-relocates-computation

A pattern can remain separate at one scale and become a composite at another. When elements shrink below a receiver’s resolution, integration moves from the medium to the receiver.

VI. Context-is-a-coefficient

Background, expectation, neighboring signals, task, history, and inferred illumination alter the meaning or appearance of an unchanged input.

[
Y=f(X\mid c)
]

VII. Combination-can-create-or-destroy-addressability

A composite may expose a property absent from its isolated components or conceal properties each component possessed alone.

VIII. Loss-is-often-the-operation

Many systems create an output by filtering, absorbing, excluding, suppressing, or intersecting. More inputs can produce less transmitted signal.

IX. Equivalent-output-is-not-equivalent-cause

When distinct inputs map to the same observation, they are cross-domain metamers.

[
X_a\neq X_b,\qquad \mathcal P(X_a)=\mathcal P(X_b)
]

Inference must preserve causal alternatives until a discriminating test separates them.

X. Unresolved-residue-is-signal

If a component cannot be translated into the shared output basis, retain it as residue. Forced conversion can erase the exact information the comparison was meant to reveal.

Canonical combination grammars

1. Additive accumulation

[
Y=\sum_i w_i x_i
]

Components contribute to a shared total.

Color origin: emitted light.

Cross-domain uses:

• pooled capital, calories, mass, votes, or inventory;
• ensemble evidence when sources are sufficiently independent;
• accumulated practice repetitions;
• additive feature models;
• multiple small forces acting in a common direction.

Characteristic failure: double-counting correlated inputs or assuming interaction-free accumulation.

2. Subtractive selection

[
Y=X_0-\bigcup_i A_i
]

The composite is what survives multiple removals.

Color origin: pigment absorption.

Cross-domain uses:

• hiring, eligibility, legal, or underwriting exclusions;
• evolutionary and market selection;
• editing and compression;
• immune filtering;
• attack-surface reduction;
• diagnostic elimination.

Characteristic failure: successive filters remove rare value along with noise.

3. Multiplicative conjunction

[
Y=X_0\prod_i t_i
]

Each stage transmits only a fraction of what enters. Serial losses compound.

Color origin: stacked optical filters.

Cross-domain uses:

• supply-chain yield across stages;
• funnel conversion;
• reliability of serial systems;
• permissions requiring every gate;
• communication through repeated lossy translations;
• probability of jointly required events.

Characteristic failure: a weak stage dominates the whole chain.

4. Intersection / all-gates-pass

[
Y=\bigcap_i X_i
]

Only mutually compatible content survives.

Cross-domain uses:

• treaty terms accepted by all jurisdictions;
• multi-constraint design feasibility;
• consensus under veto rules;
• shared semantics across languages or models;
• secure access control.

Characteristic failure: lowest-common-denominator output.

5. Spatial multiplexing and receiver-side averaging

[
Y_{\sigma}=\mathcal P_{\sigma}(x_1\parallel x_2\parallel\cdots)
]

Components remain locally distinct but integrate at another scale.

Color origin: pixels, halftones, weaving, pointillism.

Cross-domain uses:

• division of labor;
• federal or modular architectures;
• portfolio diversification;
• mixture-of-experts systems;
• ecological niches;
• parallel narratives whose global pattern emerges only at distance.

Characteristic failure: the coarse view invents homogeneity and hides local structure.

6. Temporal multiplexing and fusion

[
Y_T=\mathcal P_T(x(t))
]

Rapid alternation is integrated into an apparent continuous or simultaneous state.

Color origin: flicker fusion.

Cross-domain uses:

• time-sharing processors;
• alternating leadership;
• serialized communication that feels continuous;
• habit formation through repeated pulses;
• multiplexed radio and network traffic;
• narrative montage.

Characteristic failure: hidden discontinuity appears when timing slows or load rises.

7. Contrast / opponent differentiation

[
Y_i=x_i-\alpha N(x_i)
]

An element is intensified or altered by comparison with its neighbors or opponent channel.

Color origin: simultaneous contrast and opponent processing.

Cross-domain uses:

• price anchoring;
• political and group identity;
• figure-ground perception;
• comparative evaluation;
• branding and positioning;
• adversarial training;
• antonyms and semantic boundaries.

Characteristic failure: manufactured opposition creates polarization or a false binary.

8. Assimilation / contextual attraction

[
Y_i=x_i+\beta N(x_i)
]

Fine-grained surroundings pull an element toward the local field rather than away from it.

Color origin: chromatic assimilation.

Cross-domain uses:

• cultural normalization;
• linguistic priming;
• team norms;
• reputation spillover;
• local synchronization;
• social contagion.

Characteristic failure: conformity hides weak signals and local anomalies.

9. Masking / dominance

[
Y\approx x_k\quad\text{when}\quad w_k\gg w_{i\ne k}
]

One component prevents another from being detected without necessarily destroying it.

Color origin: intensity and saturation dominance; sensory masking analogs.

Cross-domain uses:

• loud voices in meetings;
• large market moves obscuring small signals;
• dominant features in classifiers;
• strong flavors or odors;
• headline metrics hiding distributional changes;
• acute emotion masking subtler states.

Characteristic failure: absence from output is mistaken for absence from the system.

10. Interference / phase coupling

[
Y=\left|\sum_i A_i e^{j\phi_i}\right|^2
]

Components amplify or cancel depending on relative phase and alignment.

Color origin: structural color and optical interference.

Cross-domain uses:

• synchronized teams;
• wave mechanics and signal processing;
• market cycles;
• musical harmony and beating;
• coordinated messaging;
• schedules whose effects reinforce or collide.

Characteristic failure: measuring magnitude without phase predicts the wrong composite.

11. Transduction / re-emission

[
Y=D(E(X))
]

The medium absorbs one form, transforms it internally, and emits another.

Color origin: fluorescence.

Cross-domain uses:

• enzymes and catalysts;
• sensors and actuators;
• compilers and translators;
• metaphor converting structure between semantic domains;
• trauma or experience converted into art;
• organizations converting information into action.

Characteristic failure: the emitted form is misidentified as a simple mixture of inputs.

12. Threshold / quorum phase change

[
Y=
\begin{cases}
0,&Q<\theta(c)\
1,&Q\ge\theta(c)
\end{cases}
]

Continuous accumulation crosses a boundary and becomes a qualitatively different state.

Color origin: flicker-to-fusion and detection thresholds.

Cross-domain uses:

• neural firing;
• elections and discharge petitions;
• chemical reactions;
• market breakouts;
• organizational commitment;
• perception and recognition;
• collective action.

Characteristic failure: treating the threshold as fixed when context bends the Rubicon manifold.

13. Saturation / clipping

[
Y=\min(Y_{max},f(X))
]

Additional input stops producing distinguishable output.

Cross-domain uses:

• sensor saturation;
• cognitive overload;
• advertising fatigue;
• diminishing marginal utility;
• bandwidth limits;
• API rate limits;
• emotional numbing.

Characteristic failure: interpreting a flat output as absence of additional input.

14. Metameric equivalence / equifinality

[
X_a\neq X_b,\qquad Y_a=Y_b
]

Different causal compositions generate indistinguishable surface outputs.

Color origin: different spectra perceived as the same color.

Cross-domain uses:

• identical symptoms from different mechanisms;
• identical profits from different risk structures;
• identical test scores from different understanding;
• identical text from retrieval, imitation, or reasoning;
• identical behavior from fear, habit, strategy, or belief;
• software implementations satisfying the same interface.

Characteristic failure: surface equality is promoted to ontological identity.

15. Hysteresis / path dependence

[
Y_t=f(X_t,H_{t-1})
]

The present composite depends on how the system arrived there.

Cross-domain uses:

• trust and reputation;
• magnetic and material systems;
• institutional precedent;
• learning and sensitization;
• market positioning;
• narrative character development.

Characteristic failure: reversing inputs is assumed to reverse the output.

16. Emergent interaction

[
Y=\sum_i w_i x_i+\sum_{i<j}J_{ij}x_ix_j+\cdots
]

The interaction contributes something not attributable to isolated parts.

Cross-domain uses:

• drug combinations;
• team chemistry;
• ecological symbiosis;
• feature interactions;
• humor and double meaning;
• music and harmony;
• operator formations in Pantheon.

Characteristic failure: additive accounting assigns the emergent effect to the wrong component.

