Canonical doctrine

Do not ask only, “What are the parts?” Ask:

• What carries them?
• By what algebra do they meet?
• In what arrangement and sequence?
• Who or what receives them?
• At what scale?
• Against what background?
• What survives, disappears, transforms, or newly emerges?

The world is not built from nouns alone. It is built from combination grammars. Color reveals the law because its transformations are visible, but the same operator families govern signals, minds, bodies, machines, institutions, markets, stories, and ecosystems.

Color is a verb: not a property sitting alone inside an object, but an event produced among source, medium, pattern, receiver, and world.